# Pantheon's plan, in John Carmack's .plan format (docs/PLAN.md): one item
# per line, under the day it was written. An unmarked line is open; a bid
# marks its item "+ " when done (or "* " the day it was written), "- " if
# dropped. Marked lines are pruned once they've landed, so the file holds
# what's left: `git log -p .plan` has the rest. The order is the priority.
# An item is a pointer: its details are in the files it names.

2026-11-09
shell into Soma, step 1: delete `run`. main.soma reads argv through a binding and picks the command in Soma; each command (init, keygen, genesis, edits, tx, up, plan, node) stays an OCaml function declared in shell.definition, called from Soma. All commands in one task; pass no new Soma function into OCaml. When it lands, rewrite this line for step 2 (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Next")
D-5: an agent bids the same patch twice: bid_duty doesn't count a bid in flight (sent, not yet in a block) as waiting, and a patch reply is enqueued without checking the chain; keep the in-flight transaction, drop a reply for a task the agent already has a pending bid on, with a test; e2e's bidders scenario hits it under load ("task 2 drew 3 bids", codex twice) (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-5; agent.soma bid_duty and enqueue)
D-1: the market's CI runs the tests a bid adds or changes: Pantheon's e2e scenarios, and Soma's test-suite/misc/soma_*.sh, which Soma's CI never runs; bidders can't run e2e either ("E2E blocked by sandbox socket permissions", codex, Nov 10) (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-1; PANTHEON_CI in scripts/sandbox.sh and scripts/host.sh, deploy/sandbox/soma-ci, test/e2e.sh)
P-1: the host's diplomats run with approvals off as the user that owns every node key, the inbox tokens and the push credentials: give each diplomat its own user (or VM) sharing only its inbox, run nodes and the mirror as another user with 0700 homes, keep the push key from diplomats; split it if too big (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-1; scripts/host.sh, docs/HOST.md, docs/SAFETY.md)
P-3: the Docker CI image's opam root is read-only, so a check running `soma build` fails asking opam what the switch has: a writable opam log, and the image's installed record or cache for soma; test the Docker CI path, not only ci-sandbox (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-3; deploy/pantheon-ci.sh, deploy/Dockerfile, shell.ml's docker run, deploy/sandbox/pantheon-docker)
P-13: bound a node's memory against unauthenticated and Byzantine peers: authenticate before buffering, cap buffered bytes per connection and overall, votes carry no value, cap proposals per round, cap the message log by bytes (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-13; shell.ml's connection reader, consensus.soma)
P-12: ci-sandbox binds only the /etc files a build needs, CI runs as a user that can't read node keys, and a failed check's output is redacted before it goes on chain (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-12; deploy/host/ci-sandbox, shell.ml's rejection reason)
P-11: validate the host's branch name as a git ref and quote it everywhere it reaches a shell, Python or a unit file (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-11; scripts/host.sh)
P-15: CI can't release the host-wide CI lock: close the lock's descriptor before the sandboxed command runs (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-15; deploy/host/ci-sandbox, soma-dev-build, deploy/sandbox/pantheon-docker)
D-2: model-call time limits and consensus timers use a monotonic clock, so a sleep or a clock step doesn't kill calls or fire every timer; a test that moves the wall clock under a running job (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-2; shell.ml start_job and timers)
D-3: a call the node kills takes its children with it: a process group per job, and bidder.sh ends its model on SIGTERM; a test that a killed job leaves nothing behind (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-3; shell.ml start_job, scripts/bidder.sh)
D-4: Grok Build's patches: GROK_PATCH_FLAGS with a lower default reasoning effort, then what its rejected bids share (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-4; scripts/bidder.sh)
